const GAMBIT_OPENINGS=[["evans","Evans Gambit","C52","e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Bc5 b4 Bxb4 c3 Ba5 d4 exd4 O-O Nge7 cxd4 d5 exd5 Nxd5","White gives the b-pawn to gain time for c3 and d4, building a big centre while Black's king is still in the middle. Fischer's and Kasparov's weapon.","gambit"],["danish","Danish Gambit","C21","e4 e5 d4 exd4 c3 dxc3 Bc4 cxb2 Bxb2 d5 Bxd5 Nf6 Bxf7+ Kxf7 Qxd8 Bb4+ Qd2 Bxd2+ Nxd2","White gives two pawns for two bishops raking the board. Black's best is to give material back with ...d5 and reach a calm ending.","gambit"],["smithmorra","Smith-Morra Gambit","B21","e4 c5 d4 cxd4 c3 dxc3 Nxc3 Nc6 Nf3 d6 Bc4 e6 O-O Nf6 Qe2 Be7 Rd1 e5","Against the Sicilian, White gives a pawn for quick development and open c- and d-files. Black must develop calmly and watch d6 and f7.","gambit"],["scotchgambit","Scotch Gambit","C44","e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 d4 exd4 Bc4 Nf6 e5 d5 Bb5 Ne4 Nxd4 Bd7 Bxc6 bxc6 O-O Bc5","White delays recapturing on d4 to develop the bishop with tempo against f7. Black answers with ...Nf6 and ...d5.","gambit"],["viennagambit","Vienna Gambit","C29","e4 e5 Nc3 Nf6 f4 d5 fxe5 Nxe4 Nf3 Be7 d4 O-O Bd3 f5","A King's Gambit with Nc3 played first. Black's best answer is to strike back in the centre with ...d5.","gambit"],["bdg","Blackmar-Diemer Gambit","D00","d4 d5 e4 dxe4 Nc3 Nf6 f3 exf3 Nxf3 Bg4 h3 Bxf3 Qxf3 c6 Be3 e6 Bd3 Nbd7 O-O","White gives the e-pawn to open the f-file and attack. Black develops solidly (...Bg4xf3, ...c6, ...e6) and keeps the extra pawn.","gambit"],["twoknights","Two Knights: Fried Liver lines","C59","e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bc4 Nf6 Ng5 d5 exd5 Na5 Bb5+ c6 dxc6 bxc6 Be2 h6 Nf3 e4 Ne5 Bd6","After Ng5 Black gives a pawn with 5...Na5 for fast development. Never 5...Nxd5?, which allows Nxf7, the Fried Liver.","gambit"],["marshall","Marshall Attack","C89","e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 a6 Ba4 Nf6 O-O Be7 Re1 b5 Bb3 O-O c3 d5 exd5 Nxd5 Nxe5 Nxe5 Rxe5 c6 d4 Bd6 Re1 Qh4 g3 Qh3","In the Ruy Lopez, Black gives the e-pawn for a fierce kingside attack. Born in Capablanca-Marshall, 1918; still played by the world's best.","gambit"],["schliemann","Schliemann (Jaenisch) Gambit","C63","e4 e5 Nf3 Nc6 Bb5 f5 Nc3 fxe4 Nxe4 d5 Nxe5 dxe4 Nxc6 Qg5 Qe2 Nf6 f4","Black answers the Ruy Lopez with a King's Gambit in reverse. Sharp and risky for both sides.","gambit"],["stafford","Stafford Gambit","C42","e4 e5 Nf3 Nf6 Nxe5 Nc6 Nxc6 dxc6 d3 Bc5 Be2 h5 Nd2","A trappy online favourite. Objectively bad for Black, but full of traps against f2. White should play calmly: d3, Be2, Nd2, and castle.","gambit"],["benko","Benko Gambit","A58","d4 Nf6 c4 c5 d5 b5 cxb5 a6 bxa6 g6 Nc3 Bxa6 e4 Bxf1 Kxf1 d6 Nf3 Bg7 g3 O-O Kg2","Black gives a pawn for long-term pressure on the a- and b-files and the long diagonal. Positional, not tactical.","gambit"],["budapest","Budapest Gambit","A52","d4 Nf6 c4 e5 dxe5 Ng4 Bf4 Nc6 Nf3 Bb4+ Nbd2 Qe7 a3 Ngxe5 Nxe5 Nxe5 e3 Bxd2+ Qxd2","Black hits back at once with ...e5 and wins the pawn back on e5 with active pieces. Watch for traps on d3.","gambit"],["albin","Albin Counter-Gambit","D08","d4 d5 c4 e5 dxe5 d4 Nf3 Nc6 g3 Be6 Nbd2 Qd7 Bg2 O-O-O O-O","Black's d4 pawn cramps White. The Lasker Trap punishes 4.e3?. White should develop with Nf3, g3, Bg2 and Nbd2.","gambit"],["englund","Englund Gambit","A40","d4 e5 dxe5 Nc6 Nf3 Qe7 Qd5 f6 exf6 Nxf6 Qb3","A dubious gambit that sets a famous trap (4.Bf4? Qb4+). Against it, play Nf3, guard b2, and develop. Black just ends a pawn down.","gambit"]];
